
  So the first thing to settle is what substrate we're building on, because it decides whether "notify another user" is
  even expressible.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this app has a server and real multi-user identity, or stays a single-user page in one browser.
  That's not a detail we can defer — it determines whether notifications are cross-user messages (needs a backend,
  persistence, auth, delivery) or just in-page alerts about your own data (needs none of that).

  - [1mClient-only, single user[0m — cheapest by far, no infra, works offline. Buys you: in-page toasts, maybe browser
    Notification API for due dates. Rules out: notifying [3mother[0m people, anything arriving while the tab is closed. Cheap
    to build, expensive to reverse — adding a backend later means redoing storage and identity.
  - [1mBackend, multi-user[0m — matches the literal request ("users get notified about tasks they care about"). Buys you: real
    subscriptions, cross-user events, delivery while away (email/push). Costs: a server, a datastore, auth, deployment
    — that's most of the actual work, and notifications are a small slice of it.
  - [1mClient-only now, backend-shaped seams[0m — build tasks + a local event log behind an interface that a server could
    later implement. Buys you: motion today without painting us into a corner. Costs: some abstraction you don't need
    yet, and it still can't notify anyone but you until the server exists.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third[0m, but only if you confirm multi-user is genuinely the goal. If "users" here really means
  "me, using my task list," the first option is the honest answer and the other two are YAGNI. What I want to avoid is
  designing a subscription-and-delivery system on top of an empty page.
